Proof
Consider a statistical system described by a set of \(N\) variables \(\left\{x_{i};~i =
1,2,\ldots,N\right\}\), with \(x_i \equiv x_i^{\circ}\) at the equilibrium. If the system is perturbed to a state that is not far from the equilibrium, it will relax back to it’s equilibrium state, setting up fluxes for various \(\left\{x_{i}\right\}\). Assuming that we are not far away from the equilibrium, we can write down the following linear flux-force relation \[\begin{align}\label{eq:forceflux}
    \dot{x_{i}} = \Gamma_{ij} X_{j},
\end{align}\] we will shortly describe what \(X_{j}\) are. We are interested in calculating average values of various quantities, for example \(x_{i}\), \(x_{i} X_{j}\). Since we are not far from equilibrium and the system is relaxing towards its equilibrium state, we can compute these averages using the machinery of equilibrium statistical mechanics. Essentially, the probability of a fluctuation which takes the system out of equilibrium is proportional to the number of microstates accessible by that fluctuation i.e. \[ P(\left\{x_{i}\right\}) \propto \Omega(\left\{x_{i}\right\}).\] Using the Boltzmann relation \(S = k \ln{\Omega}\), we get \(P\left(\left\{x_{i}\right\}\right) \propto e^{\Delta S/k}\) where \(\Delta S\) is the change in entropy. We have \[\begin{align}
    \Delta S &= S\left(x_{i}\right) - S\left(x^{\circ}_{i}\right) \\
             &= \left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial x_{i}}\right)_{x_{i}=x_{i}^{\circ}}\Delta x_{i} \\
             &+ \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\partial^{2} S}{\partial x_{i}x_{j}}\right)_{x_{i,j}=x_{i,j}^{\circ}}\Delta x_{i} \Delta x_{j},
\end{align}\] where \(\Delta x_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i}^{\circ}\). Since the entropy is maximum at the equilibrium, the first derivative vanishes. We then have \[\begin{align}\label{eq:deltaS}
    \Delta S &= -\beta_{ij}\Delta x_{i} \Delta x_{j},
\end{align}\] where we have defined \[\begin{align}
    \beta_{ij} = -\beta_{ji} \equiv \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\partial^{2} S}{\partial x_{i}x_{j}}\right)_{x_{i,j}=x_{i,j}^{\circ}}
\end{align}\] From \(\eqref{eq:deltaS}\), the driving force is defined as \[\begin{align}
    X_{i} = \lim_{\Delta x_{i} \rightarrow 0} \frac{\Delta S}{\Delta x_{i}} = - \beta_{ij}\Delta x_{j}
\end{align}\]
We have Q: Why can you still use the Boltzmann average here? \[\begin{align}
    \left&lt; x_{i} X_{j}\right&gt;=\frac{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x_{i} X_{j} e^{\Delta S /k}\prod_{i} d x_{i}}{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{\Delta S /k} \prod_{i} d x_{i}}
\end{align}\] \[\begin{align}\label{eq:avexi}
    \left&lt; x_{i}\right&gt;=x_{i}^{\circ}=\frac{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x_{i} e^{\Delta S /k}\prod_{i} d x_{i}}{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{\Delta S /k} \prod_{i} d x_{i}}
\end{align}\] Differentiating \(\eqref{eq:avexi}\) with respect to \(x_{j}^{\circ}\) we arrive at
\[\left&lt; x_{i} X_{j}\right&gt;=-k \delta_{ij}\]
Q: What is the point of time flow when it is irreversible. Or reversible? From time-reversibility and homogeneity we have
\[\begin{align}\label{eq:avexi}
    \left&lt;x_{i}(t)x_{j}(t+s)\right&gt;
        &= \left&lt;x_{i}(t)x_{j}(t-s) \right&gt; \\
        &= \left&lt;x_{i}(t+s)x_{j}(t) \right&gt;
\end{align}\]
Subtracting \(\left&lt;x_{i}(t)x_{j}(t)\right&gt;\) from both sides and taking the limit \(s\rightarrow0\) we get the following
\[\begin{align}
    \lim_{s\rightarrow0}\left&lt;x_{i}(t)\left[x_{j}(t+s)-x_{j}(t)\right]\right&gt;
    &= \lim_{s\rightarrow0}\left&lt;\left[x_{i}(t+s)-x_{i}(t)\right]x_{j}(t)\right&gt; \\
    \left&lt;x_{i}(t)\dot{x_{j}}(t)\right&gt; &= \left&lt;\dot{x_{i}}(t)x_{j}(t) \right&gt; \\
\end{align}\] Using \(\eqref{eq:forceflux}\) we arrive at \[\begin{align}\label{eq:trsresult}
    \Gamma_{jl}\left&lt;x_{i}(t)X_{l}(t)\right&gt; &= \Gamma_{il}\left&lt;x_{j}(t)X_{l}(t)\right&gt; \\
\end{align}\]
Time reversibility will only take us this far, to proceed further, we need to compute \(\left&lt;x_{i}(t)X_{j}(t)\right&gt;\).Using the above expression in \(\eqref{eq:trsresult}\) we get \[\begin{align}\label{eq:avexi}
    \Gamma_{ij} = \Gamma_{ji},
\end{align}\] which are the Onsager Reciprocal relations.

Onsager Symmetry relations as a Fluctuation-Dissipation Theorem
Since the Symmetry of the kinetic coefficients is derived from the fundamental hypothesis of the equilibrium statistical mechanics. It can be viewed as a relation between the systematic behaviour of the system to a Fluctuation with the random part. To elaborate, the admittance is derived from the properties of the fluctuations.


Minimal way to break ORR
Let’s add general linear terms to the equations of motion of \(\phi_{1}\) and \(\phi_{2}\) \[\begin{align}\label{eq:fe}
    \dot{\phi_{1}} &= K \nabla^{2}\phi_{1} - \left(a + b \phi_{1}^{2}\right)\phi_{1} - \chi \phi_{2} + A \phi_{1} + B \phi_{2}\\
    \dot{\phi_{2}} &= K \nabla^{2}\phi_{2} - \left(a + b \phi_{2}^{2}\right)\phi_{2} - \chi \phi_{1} + C \phi_{1} + D \phi_{2}.
\end{align}\] Since all our coefficients are phenomenological, we can combine the \(A,D\) terms with the \(a\) terms and rewrite the equations as \[\begin{align}\label{eq:fe}
    \dot{\phi_{1}} &= K \nabla^{2}\phi_{1} - \left(a_{1} + b \phi_{1}^{2}\right)\phi_{1} + \left(-\chi + B\right) \phi_{2} \\
    \dot{\phi_{2}} &= K \nabla^{2}\phi_{2} - \left(a_{2} + b \phi_{2}^{2}\right)\phi_{2} + \left(-\chi + C\right) \phi_{1},
\end{align}\] where \(a_{1} = a + A\) and \(a_{2} = a + D\). Further, rewriting in terms of \(\overline{\chi} = -\chi + \frac{B}{2} +
\frac{C}{2}\) and \(\alpha = \frac{B}{2} - \frac{C}{2}\), we get \[\begin{align}\label{eq:fe}
    \dot{\phi_{1}} &= K \nabla^{2}\phi_{1} + \left(a_{1} + b \phi_{1}^{2}\right)\phi_{1} + \left(\overline{\chi} + \alpha\right) \phi_{2} \\
    \dot{\phi_{2}} &= K \nabla^{2}\phi_{2} + \left(a_{2} + b \phi_{2}^{2}\right)\phi_{2} + \left(\overline{\chi} - \alpha\right) \phi_{1}.
\end{align}\] Notice that the structure of the terms except the \(\alpha\) terms is exactly identical to \(\eqref{eq:phi_dynamics}\). Thus, the minimal way to break the ORR relations for systems which are amenable to a Landau-Ginzburg like free energy description is by adding antisymmetric terms.
